  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nA low pressure system over central Indiana will continue to \nslowly move east through the Ohio Valley today. Rain continues \nto move north across the forecast area on the northeast side of \nthis system. Meanwhile, some persistent rain showers are present\nin southern Michigan and northern Indiana in a deformation zone\nwith this low, immediately with the upper trough axis. This \nband of precipitation will be the first concern of the day as it\npivots toward the Toledo metro area. Aloft, a coupled jet \nsituation will develop with a left exit region of the southerly \njet and the right entrance region of the northerly jet align \nover northern Ohio later today. This will enhance the lift \nacross the region and allow for this band of rain to become \nheavier with more efficient precipitation rates. Portions of \nNorthwest Ohio will get 1 to 2 inches of rainfall by this \nafternoon and some areas could receive higher amounts where rain\npersists. Elsewhere, a band of moderate to heavy rain continues\nmoving northeast through northern Ohio early this morning. This\nband is progressing well and a dry slot is developing over \ncentral Ohio and moving into the forecast area. This will allow \nfor some recovery before new showers and storms develop later \nthis morning into the afternoon. Rainfall in NE OH and NW PA \nwill be around an inch with locally higher amounts possible, \nespecially in convective cores that develop this afternoon. With\nheavy rain potential for several portions of the forecast area,\nhave gone ahead and issued a Flood Watch for the entire area. \nThe Flood Watch for Northwest Ohio will start now and go through\n5 PM this afternoon. For the rest of the area, the Flood Watch \nwill start at 10 AM this morning and go through Midnight \ntonight. \n\nThe other forecast concern for today will be the potential for \nstrong to severe thunderstorms. This will mainly be an issue for the \neastern half of the forecast area, as the western half will likely \ncontinue to be worked over by heavy rain this morning. As the \nsurface low moves across northern Ohio today and the coupled jet \naloft provides ample lift, new showers and storms should develop \nduring the late morning and early afternoon hours. A break in \nconvective activity over the eastern half of the forecast area \nshould allow for some instability to build, especially if we can get \na couple breaks in the clouds and get temperatures in the lower 80s. \nThe most likely severe weather threat would be strong to damaging \nwind gusts in some wet microbursts. However, given the shear in the \nregion from the surface low and upper trough moving through and some \nslightly backed flow ahead of the low, there is a non-zero tornado \nthreat this afternoon.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nThe upper trough from the system moving through the region \ntoday will amplify and form a cut off upper low. The trends in \nthis upper low are much further south over the past couple of \nforecast cycles. This then depicts some upper ridging over the \nlocal area as surface high pressure enters the region from the \nnorth. The result is a drier forecast for the area on Monday and\nTuesday with generally seasonable temperatures. On Wednesday, \nthe upper low will get absorbed into an upper trough moving \nthrough the Great Lakes region. This will mean rain chances will\nreturn on Wednesday, especially areas south and east. The upper\ntrough pattern will remain over the region through the end of \nthe week and provide support for daily, scattered rain chances. \nTemperatures will remain near normal.",
